  "query_preview": "/Thursday through Monday/...    \nAnother pronounced trough with strong cyclonic flow will propagate \ninto the western Conus from the East Pacific during the latter half \nof the week. However, trends in both operational and ensemble \nmembers suggest the main cold core and negative height anomalies \neven further north than previous iterations. This would place the \njet streak only into far NW Arizona while a decaying cold front \nessentially loses definition crossing the coastal range resulting in \nlittle to no forcing mechanisms into the forecast area. Not \nsurprisingly, modest POPs in prior forecasts have mostly been \neliminated. However, forecast confidence is excellent that this \ntrough passage will reinforce temperatures 3F-6F below normal \nthrough the weekend, and potentially result in the first hard freeze \nof the season for some mid-elevation locations in Gila County and \nthroughout Joshua Tree NP. Some frost would also be likely in the \nmore rural, sheltered river valleys over the weekend. \n\nSubstantial ensemble spread is maintained early next week in a \npattern characterized by northern stream blocking, but a strong \nsouthern stream subtropical jet and progressive flow. GEFS members \nare increasingly trending towards a deepening wave tapping better \nquality moisture over the SW Conus early next week, while the CMC \nand EPS suites are far more disparate and trending towards a lower \namplitude, less impactful system. NBM output seems to be weighted \nmore towards the majority of the EPS/CMC idea with very low POPs \ncentered only over higher terrain areas. However, given the strength \nof the current El Nino episode and intensity of the subtropical jet, \na stronger and more significant weather disturbance cannot be \ndiscounted.",
